    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hyampom’s name comes from the Wintu word xaayin‑pom, meaning “slippery place,” and the area was once home to the Whilkut, Chimariko, and Northern Wintun tribes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyampom%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Jedediah Strong Smith first explored Hyampom in April 1828, marking the area’s earliest recorded European encounter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyampom%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hyampom sits at 1,503 feet in the Trinity Mountains, tucked into a small valley surrounded by rugged peaks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyampom%2C_California",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Despite its low elevation, Hyampom receives only about 6.7 inches of snow annually, far less than nearby high‑altitude stations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyampom%2C_California",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A modest airstrip on the outskirts lets small planes touch down, a relic of the town’s early aviation days.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyampom%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The South Fork Trinity River and Hayfork Creek flow through Hyampom, offering quiet spots for fishing and kayaking.",
